estment firms Other CNB statistics Bank Lending Survey Research Research at CNB CNB research priorities CNB research economists Research publications Working paper Research and Policy Notes Research Briefs CNB Research News Conferences, workshops and seminars International Collaboration ČNB Lab AI & Data Science Digital Assets Pilot Payments User section News archive For Media Contact us Complaints FAQ Search čeština FX rate declared by the CNB for&nbsp;24/4/2026 EUR - 24.370 CZK USD - 20.802 CZK All FX rates declared by the CNB 2025: Inflation at target and a record return on international reserves The year 2025 was marked by low inflation, which has held close to the Czech National Bank’s (CNB) inflation target for more than two years. The central bank also achieved the highest-ever return on its international reserves: a record CZK 253 billion (a year-on-year gain of 10.3%). The gradual adjustment of the composition of the reserves is paying off – over the past three years, the CNB has achieved an accumulated profit of CZK 133 billion. As of 20 March this year, it was operating with an interim profit of CZK 95 billion. The interim result was positively affected by exchange rate movements and returns on international reserves. Press release Financial Report (Czech version) Inflation rises to 1.9% in March due to the impacts of the conflict in the Middle East CNB instant payments save businesses time and money, yet only one in five currently use QR codes at the point of sale Minutes of the Bank Board Meeting on 19 March 2026 News 21. 4. 2026 Bank lending survey – II/2026 Credit standards for loans to corporations and for housing loans to households remained broadly unchanged in 2026 Q1, while those for consumer credit eased. Demand for loans among households and corporations rose further. The continued increase in demand for housing loans was driven mainly by expectations of further growth in&nbsp;residential property prices. News 16. 4. 2026 CNB WP 8/2026 – Hedging Against Inflation: International Evidence on Investor Clientele Effects Recent work published as a CNB Working Paper shows that European investors have sharply increased their use of inflation-linked bonds, reflecting a growing focus on inflation risk in the post-pandemic period. Using bond-level holdings data from 2013-2023, the authors document strong clientele patterns: pension funds rely heavily on inflation-linked securities, insurers largely avoid them, and investors overwhelmingly prefer bonds tied to their own domestic inflation index. The study demonstrates how regulatory changes can swiftly reshape pension funds’ demand for inflation hedging. News 31. 3. 2026 International Monetary Fund (IMF) Mission Report The CNB has published the final report from the results of the IMF’s Article IV consultation which assesses the state of the Czech economy every year. News 30. 3. 2026 Financial market inflation expectations – March 2026 Fourteen domestic and two foreign analysts took part in the March survey of financial market inflation expectations. The data obtained indicate that some analysts are now beginning to reflect rising oil and fuel prices in their one-year inflation forecasts. However, the increase is modest. Inflation expectations at the three-year horizon remain anchored and the forecast is unchanged. The outlook for domestic economic growth this year and next year has also remained unchanged. (pdf,&nbsp;1&nbsp;MB) News 30. 3. 2026 CNB WP 7/2026 – Euroisation and the Bank Lending Channel of Monetary Policy: Evidence from Czechia Using AnaCredit loan-level data for Czechia from 2019 to 2024, we find that a wider interest rate differential is associated with a shift in new lending to non-financial corporations from koruna to euro-denominated loans, especially at large, less-capitalised banks and for larger, lower-leverage firms. The shift appears stronger when the differential widens than when it narrows, suggesting only a partial move back to koruna lending. Press releases 25. 3. 2026 The CNB sells its building in Plzeň The Czech National Bank (CNB) has completed the sale of its building in Plzeň. It will be acquired by the construction company V.D.O. Group s.r.o., which offered the highest price (CZK 102.5 million). The sale of the property is part of the CNB’s long-term efforts to manage its assets effectively, optimise operating costs and modernise services for clients. The Bank Board decided on the sale on 8 January 2026. Read CNB Governor Aleš Michl’s selected speeches and interviews in which he explains how the central bank is fighting inflation and what steps it is taking to achieve price stability. Consumer prices in the Czech Republic are going up at a rate of around 2% a year. Maintaining low, stable and thus predictable inflation is the primary objective of the Czech National Bank. The CNB Lab innovation hub is a platform that brings together the central bank’s activities in the area of innovation and modern technologies. It provides a single point of access to information on projects focused on artificial intelligence, digital assets testing and the future of payments. By 2028, the Czech National Bank will hold 100 tonnes of gold – the most in its history – in its international reserves, gradually rebuilding the nation’s gold reserves. Why gold? Payments are an essential part of our daily lives, and instant payments allow you to send money without the wait. How exactly do they work, and how can they make your life easier? Read more to find out. The CNB has prepared a web portal devoted to its financial and economic literacy activities for the public. Key rates 2.50% Discount rate 3.50% 2W repo rate 4.50% Lombard rate More about rates Inflation 1.9% March 2026 More about inflation Current CNB forecast 1.6% Inflation 2026 2.1% Inflation 2027 2.9% GDP 2026 2.9% GDP 2027 More about forecast Capital buffer rates 1.25% CCyB rate 0.5% SyRB rate 2.5% O-SIIB max rate More about rates Limits on credit ratios 80%